<div class="r-site" data-r107-page data-family="about" data-modules="1,2,3,4,5"><figure class="r-about-campaign"><img src="{{ 'suiji-home-about-optimized.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="SUIJI design campaign concept" width="1200" height="1400" loading="eager" decoding="async" class="" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Studio campaign concept</figcaption></figure><header class="r-intro"><p class="r-eyebrow">About SUIJI</p><h1>Jewelry that changes with you.</h1><p>Our story began in bespoke fine jewelry in 2014, with handmade pieces and conversations about the lives they would become part of. SUIJI grew from those exchanges: a way to keep the details you love, take part in the design and find new ways to wear them.</p></header><section class="r-about-chapter"><h2>What SUIJI means</h2><div>
<p>SUIJI expresses the idea of following yourself. Your jewelry can make room for the details you have always loved, the parts of yourself you want to express and the things you are still discovering.</p>
<p>Age, gender and other labels do not decide who a design is for. We want everyone to have a part in designing jewelry that feels unmistakably their own.</p>

</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="why-modular"><h2>Why structure</h2><div>
<p>In our bespoke work, a customer might imagine a favorite design as earrings, a ring or a necklace. Another might want to change one small detail for an outfit or an evening out. Buying a whole new piece each time meant buying parts they already owned and still enjoyed.</p>
<p>Those conversations became the starting point for our modular approach. The most demanding design work lies in the structure: how the pieces connect and which elements can work together. We work through those questions so you have more room to choose: keep a base you love, swap in a compatible charm and explore a different combination.</p>
<p>We want jewelry to be used and enjoyed often. A special occasion can be where a piece begins, with many ordinary days still to come.</p>

<p><strong>Designed to be worn.</strong> We design with the body in mind, considering proportions, contact, balance and movement as well as appearance.</p></div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="engineering"><h2>Core-Lock &amp; Modular System</h2><div>
<p>Bases give the jewelry its form and wearing position. Charms bring color, texture and personal meaning. Core-Lock is the SUIJI multi-use clasp that connects compatible elements.</p>
<p>SUIJI charms are designed for bases equipped with the corresponding SUIJI clasp. Bare hoops and other components may need an additional attachment. Check the included parts and compatibility on each piece page; one charm occupies one position at a time.</p>
<p>During the later development of Core-Lock, we worked with French engineers specializing in springs to refine the spring structure, mechanics and the pressure needed to operate the clasp.</p>
<p>The clasp design was granted a Chinese utility model patent: ZL 2020 2 0983805.3. This engineering work supports the small, everyday gesture at the heart of SUIJI: changing a detail while keeping the piece you love.</p>
<p><a href="https://patents.google.com/patent/CN212545896U/en">Read the patent publication · CN212545896U</a></p>

</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="designer"><h2>A note from Chunyu</h2><div class="r-designer-lead"><figure class="r-designer-portrait"><img src="{{ 'suiji-designer-chunyu-wen-v40.jpeg' | asset_url }}" alt="Chunyu Wen — SUIJI Brand &amp; Product" width="1230" height="1672" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class=""></figure><div class="r-designer-lead__copy"><p class="r-designer-role">Chunyu Wen · Founder, Brand &amp; Product</p><p>When I think about how I came to make jewelry, the memories that come back first are from childhood—long before university, or the thought of having a studio of my own.</p><p>I was born at a mine, where looking for gemstones was one of our childhood games. Some of those stones sold for high prices elsewhere, but we played with them as casually as other children played with pebbles. Their colors, their curious shapes, the pleasure of finding one I liked… that was what mattered to me. I had no idea what they were worth.</p></div></div><div class="r-designer-story"><p>It was only much later that I made the connection with my name: Chunyu means “spring jade.” Such a small detail, present all along, though I’d never thought about it that way. Sometimes I wonder whether I was always meant to find my way to jewelry.</p><p>I didn’t take a straight path to it. I studied visual communication at Tsinghua University’s Academy of Arts &amp; Design, then worked in UI/UX design, branding and marketing. Later, I went to France for postgraduate studies in brand management at INSEEC. After graduating, I spent many years working in France and the United States, across different sectors and in roles that included management.</p><p>Those years taught me a great deal, yet jewelry never quite left my thoughts. Memories kept returning, along with the wish to give that early interest a place in my working life. Eventually, I had to ask myself: what if I gave myself the chance to make jewelry my work?</p><p>When life became a little more settled, I finally felt ready to find out. I left the work I was doing, opened my own studio and began creating bespoke fine jewelry. My earlier experiences proved invaluable. The years in branding, marketing and management helped me build the business and gave me the confidence to keep going.</p><p>Over the past decade, I’ve worked with clients whose needs were as varied as their tastes. Some regularly attended very different kinds of events; others had a history of allergies that we needed to take into account. We took the time to understand each person’s life and preferences, and designed their pieces with those details in mind. Many of those clients have become friends. A shared love of jewelry has given us plenty to keep talking about, long after the original commission.</p><p>I later founded SUIJI to bring that experience of personal choice to more people: the pleasure of choosing the details, taking part in the design and seeing something of yourself in the result.</p><p>That pleasure in choosing and combining things is something I recognize at home, too. My husband is French, and I now divide my time between France and China. Even as a little girl, our daughter paid close attention to how the colors in her clothes and jewelry went together. I still see that same care in the way she dresses for different occasions, making small choices that are very much her own.</p></div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="studio"><h2>The studio</h2><div>
<p>Our small team brings different lives and different connections to jewelry into the studio. Chunyu Wen leads Brand &amp; Product; Yue Wang works on AI, automation and marketing. Personal stories and the way someone wants to wear a piece remain the starting point.</p>
<p>Designers, engineers, master artisans and experienced makers work together on design and technology. We draw on traditional handcraft and intangible cultural heritage techniques while exploring contemporary methods such as advanced 3D printing. Together, we refine forms, connections and the details that make a piece feel right.</p>
<p>This is how we connect the skills of the past with the possibilities of the future. Keeping a craft alive means continuing to practice it, share it and give it new possibilities. We want that care to carry into the life of the jewelry, too.</p>

</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="materials"><h2>Materials and continued use</h2><div>
<p>We use titanium alloy for the parts designed to sit against the skin. Other materials are chosen to suit the design and the way each piece is made, with lightness guiding every choice. We consider the complete piece on the body: its contact points, proportions, balance and movement.</p>
<p>Handmade textures and surface details give each piece its own character. Materials, dimensions, function and important details are confirmed for the specific design; a photograph does not replace that information.</p>
<p>Our approach to sustainability begins with keeping the pieces you love in use. Keep a base, change a compatible charm or carry a favorite detail into another combination. A new occasion need not mean buying the same components again.</p>
<p>Care matters alongside design. Clean, store and check your jewelry in a way that suits its components. If something needs attention, talk to us about the piece and the repair possibilities before setting it aside.</p>
<p><a href="{{ routes.root_url | append: '/pages/care-guide' | replace: '//', '/' }}">Explore care guidance</a> · <a href="{{ routes.root_url | append: '/pages/warranty' | replace: '//', '/' }}">Ask about care and repair</a></p>

</div></section><section class="r-about-chapter" id="mission"><h2>Our mission</h2><div>
<p>Our mission is to give everyone a part in designing jewelry that feels entirely their own, and to make those pieces a lasting part of everyday life.</p>
<p>As life changes, what we ask of jewelry changes too: how it feels to wear, what suits an occasion and what we want it to express. A piece chosen at one stage of life may need a different detail to feel right at the next. We want our designs to leave room for those changes.</p>
<p>Jewelry can become a familiar companion through new challenges and the moments that matter: a first day in a new job, a hard-earned achievement, an engagement or a wedding. A piece can hold something of that experience, the people beside you or a promise you want to remember.</p>
<p>When the occasion has passed, we hope it remains a piece you reach for often, gathering new memories. You may find different ways to wear it over the years without losing what first made it special. That is what we want for SUIJI: meaning that stays with you, and a design that has room to grow with your life.</p>
